---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Badge from '../components/Badge.astro';
import { getTools } from '../lib/load';

const tools = getTools();
const dead = tools.filter((t) => t.status !== 'alive');
const alive = tools.filter((t) => t.status === 'alive');
---

<Base title="The AI Graveyard — is that tool still alive? · HELPINhex" description="AI tools die constantly. How to spot a dead one before you build your project on it.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 style="font-size:2.2rem">🪦 The AI Graveyard</h1>
      <p class="muted">
        AI tools die at a brutal rate: the company pivots, the repo goes quiet, the domain expires,
        the free tier becomes a paid wall. Nothing on this page is drama — it's maintenance reality.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-3">Current status of our catalog</h2>
      {
        dead.length > 0 ? (
          <div class="grid wide">
            {dead.map((t) => (
              <div class="card">
                <div class="flex-between"><h3 style="margin:0">{t.name}</h3><Badge tone="warn">{t.status}</Badge></div>
                <p class="muted small">{t.tagline}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        ) : (
          <div class="card">
            <p style="margin:0">✓ Every tool in the HELPinhex catalog is currently <strong>alive</strong> ({alive.length}/{tools.length}).
            When one dies, it moves here — visibly, with its verified date. Directories never tell you when a tool died; we consider that the actual job.</p>
          </div>
        )
      }

      <h2 class="mt-3">Check any tool yourself (the 60-second autopsy)</h2>
      <div class="card">
        <ol class="howto">
          <li><strong>GitHub?</strong> Open the repo → last commit date. 6+ months of silence on an "active" AI tool is a red flag; 2+ years is a tombstone.</li>
          <li><strong>Links?</strong> Click the official site, the docs, and the pricing page. Dead domains and 404 docs = the maintainer moved on.</li>
          <li><strong>Changelog/release notes?</strong> A tool with no release in a year is in maintenance mode at best. In AI, no updates ≈ falling behind monthly.</li>
          <li><strong>Community pulse?</strong> Check the issues/Discord/reddit. Unanswered issues and a silent Discord are the smell of abandonment.</li>
          <li><strong>Google it + "discontinued" or "shut down".</strong> Sometimes the death is announced and nobody told the directories.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted small" style="margin-bottom:0">
          Famous example: Inflection's consumer chatbot <em>Pi</em> — heavily covered in 2023–24, then the company pivoted to enterprise work and the consumer product faded.
          Directories kept listing it. Users kept finding it. That gap is exactly why this page exists.
        </p>
      </div>

      <blockquote class="mt-3">
        <strong>Why this matters for beginners:</strong> you don't lose money when a tool dies — you lose the weekend you spent learning its interface,
        the workflow you built around it, and the muscle memory. Choose tools the way the Repo Radar does: recent activity is the vital sign.
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
